1 OCCU Professor i Fysik. Antiquarius Regni, Antiquarius Regni of National Heritage
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Jakob Isthmén was born in Uppsala in 1644, as the son of Isak Isthmenius and Anna Gestricia. He probably grew up at Broby manor in Börje parish, just outside the city, which was owned by the family, and which he la
2 CONC ter inherited. In May 1649 he was enroled at Uppsala University, where his father was professor in physics. In 1658 his father died, and his mother married to the renowned scholar in ancient Norse history, Olof Verelius, who was t
2 CONC o have a huge influence on his stepson's future in academic studies and profession.
2 CONT
2 CONT It seems that Isthmén spent his youth studying, assisting his stepfather in the studies of old Icelandic scripture, together with the Icelander Jonas Rugman. Through the help of Rugman, Verelius and Isthmén acquired an invaluable 
2 CONC collection of old Icelandic scriptures. In the summer of 1666 he was suggested by Johan Hadorph, a prominent scholar who would soon be appointed assessor at the government agency for antiquities, to be his assistant on a planned s
2 CONC cientific journey through Sweden, which however never materialised. The relationship between Hadorph and Isthmén would soon deteriorate drastically.
2 CONT
2 CONT In November 1666 Olof Verelius was appointed the ''Antiquarius Regni'', State Antiquarian, with Hadorph as his second in charge. Hadorph had himself shown interest in this position, but accepted to be subordinate to the senior Ver
2 CONC elius. When Verelius a few years later made it clear that he wanted to retire from his position, however, he made it through a petition to the King, to pass his office to his stepson, Jakob Isthmén. The substantially more merited 
2 CONC Johan Hadorph did everything in his power to prevent this appointment, but forces led by Verelius and Olof Rudbeck were able to force Verelius' request through. Thus on September 30 1775 Jakob Isthmén was appointed the new Antiqua
2 CONC rius Regni, by a letter from the King. Two weeks later, on October 13, he was raised into nobility, and took the name Reenhielm. His family was introduced at the House of Lords in 1678.
2 CONT
2 CONT Reenhielm's time as Antiquarius Regni was to be problematic. In April 1669 he had married Anna Böllja, the daughter of the early deceased Kettil Böllja and Sophia Brautmüller. Her uncle, Claes Stensson Böllja, had in 1665 died chi
2 CONC ldless as the last male member of this noble family, which laid the foundation of a long dispute about the inheritance between the widow and Reenhielm, which was to last for virtually the remainder of his life. The court of appeal
2 CONC  had in May 1674 made a resolution in favour of the widow. Now came the conflict with Johan Hadorph, who could not accept Reenhielm as his superior. He thus did everything he could to render his work difficult. In addition Reenhie
2 CONC lm never received his salary of 600 Daler in silver annually, which he was entitled to through his office. Apparently he accomplished very little in his time as the State Antiquarian.
2 CONT
2 CONT After less the three years at the office, in April 1678 Reenhielm entered Gustaf Cruus' enlisted cavalry regiment as lieutenant, in the ongoing war against Denmark. He took part in the regiment's operations in Norway, but less tha
2 CONC n a year later he had left the military service. It is probable that he entered the military service for economic reasons. It is clear that he did not intend to retire from his office as State Antiquarian. However, Johan Hadorph t
2 CONC ook the chance in his absence to acquire his position, and although he was never formally appointed, he was soon widely accepted as the new Antiquarius Regni. Reenhielm struggled for many years to regain his position, stating the 
2 CONC facts that he had never resigned from it, neither been sacked, and that Hadorph had no letter of appointment by the King. In March 1681 he requested that his salaries for the years 1675 - 1680 were to be paid to him. His struggles
2 CONC  were in vain. For some time he also tried to get a position at the military staff, but again without any success.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jakob Reenhielm was a serious student of the old Norse scriptures, and could together with Olof Verelius be regarded as a pioneer in this area. He was, however, of a much less stature than his stepfather Verelius and his great ant
2 CONC agonist Johan Hadorph. His views were heavily influenced by Verelius, in particular the view that the old Norse sagas were all depicting true events. He is remembered for suggesting that the apparently unrealistic feats of the her
2 CONC oes of the sagas, were to be ascribed to the fact that people during the Viking era were simply eating and exercising better than his contemporaries! In Olof Rudbeck, who was one of the most influential personalities of the era, a
2 CONC nd who was a friend and former student of Olof Verelius, he had an ally, who gave him some steadfast support. His possibilities to carry out research were however hampered by Johan Hadorph, who for among other things used his powe
2 CONC r to block Reenhielm's access to the national archives, even long after the war between them had been won by Hadorph. Hadorph was also heading a legal process against Reenhielm, stating that his acquisitions of Icelandic scripture
2 CONC s had been illegal, and that he should turn them over to the State. Reenhielm's bitterness towards Hadorph is evident in his letters. Arguably he received an undeservedly poor reputation by him. Finally Reenhielm were able to brin
2 CONC g out two books in his lifetime: In 1680 he published ''Torsten Vikingssons saga'' in translation and with notes in Latin, followed in 1691 by ''Olof Tryggvassons saga''. Much later, in 1729, his ''Observationes brevissimae ad Chr
2 CONC onicon rhytmicum S. Olai, Norrigiae regis, cura Joh. Hadorphi editum'' were printed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jakob Reehielm died in late autumn of 1691 at his estate in Täby in Vänge parish, outside Uppsala. On March 17 the following year he was buried at the Holy Trinity churh in Uppsala, where his crest was placed. He was survived by h
2 CONC is wife and their six daughters.
